hese three points are exactly the set \\(S\\); by Lemma 1 no sunny line can contain two of them. Hence the two sunny lines can cover at most two of the three points, leaving one uncovered. Contradiction.\n\nThus a non‑sunny line covering three points cannot work.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.  Non‑sunny line covering **two** points  \n\nThe possibilities are  \n\n* vertical \\(x=2\\) (covers \\((2,1),(2,2)\\)),  \n* horizontal \\(y=2\\) (covers \\((1,2),(2,2)\\)),  \n* diagonal \\(x+y=3\\) (covers \\((1,2),(2,1)\\)).\n\n*Case \\(x=2\\).*  \nRemaining points: \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\).  \nThe only admissible pair among them is \\((1,2)-(3,1)\\) whose slope is \\(-\\tfrac12\\) (sunny).  \nThus one sunny line can cover \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\).  \nThe two points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) lie on the same vertical line \\(x=1\\), which is non‑sunny; consequently they cannot be covered together by a sunny line. The second sunny line can cover at most one of them, leaving the other uncovered. Contradiction.\n\n*Case \\(y=2\\).*  \nRemaining points: \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \nThe only admissible sunny pair is \\((1,3)-(2,1)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)).  \nAfter covering this pair, the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) are horizontal, so they cannot be together on a sunny line. Again one point stays uncovered – contradiction.\n\n*Case \\(x+y=3\\).*  \nRemaining points: \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nThe only sunny pair is \\((1,1)-(2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \nThe two points \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) have slope \\(-1\\) and therefore cannot be on a sunny line together. One of them remains uncovered – contradiction.\n\nHence a non‑sunny line covering exactly two points is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.  Non‑sunny line covering **one** point  \n\nIf the non‑sunny line contains only a single point of \\(T_3\\), the two sunny lines must cover the remaining five points.  \nBy Lemma 2 each sunny line can contain at most two points, so together they can cover at most four points, leaving at least one point uncovered. Contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince every possible type of non‑sunny line leads to a contradiction, **no** arrangement of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines can satisfy the covering condition for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with }k=2\\text{ sunny lines does not exist.}}\n\\]"
