------------------------------------\nSince exactly two of the three lines must be sunny, the third line is non‑sunny.  \nA non‑sunny line is either horizontal \\(y=c\\), vertical \\(x=c\\), or diagonal \\(x+y=c\\).  \nWe examine all possibilities according to the number of points of \\(T_3\\) that such a line can contain.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Non‑sunny line covering three points  \n\nThe only non‑sunny lines with three points of \\(T_3\\) are  \n\n* \\(x=1\\) (covers \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\)),  \n* \\(y=1\\) (covers \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\)),  \n* \\(x+y=4\\) (covers \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\)).\n\n*Case \\(x=1\\).*  \nRemaining points: \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nEvery pair among them is vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\); thus no sunny line can contain two of them.  \nWith only two sunny lines left, at most two of the three points can be covered, leaving one uncovered – contradiction.\n\n*Case \\(y=1\\).*  \nRemaining points: \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\).  \nAgain each pair has a forbidden slope, so the two sunny lines can cover at most two of the three points – contradiction.\n\n*Case \\(x+y=4\\).*  \nRemaining points: \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) = \\(S\\).  \nBy Lemma 1 no sunny line can contain two of these points; the two sunny lines can cover at most two points – contradiction.\n\nHence a non‑sunny line covering three points cannot work.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Non‑sunny line covering two points  \n\nThe possibilities are  \n\n* \\(x=2\\) (covers \\((2,1),(2,2)\\)),  \n* \\(y=2\\) (covers \\((1,2),(2,2)\\)),  \n* \\(x+y=3\\) (covers \\((1,2),(2,1)\\)).\n\n*Case \\(x=2\\).*  \nRemaining points: \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\).  \nThe only sunny pair among them is \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  \nOne sunny line can cover this pair.  \nThe two leftover points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\), which is