            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most two points.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points\n\\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\).\nAmong the three numbers \\(a_i\\) at least two are equal or among the three numbers\n\\(b_i\\) at least two are equal, or among the three numbers \\(a_i+b_i\\) at least two are equal\n(pigeon‑hole principle, three objects, three “boxes’’).  \nIf two points share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, \\(L\\) is vertical; if they share the same\n\\(y\\)-coordinate, \\(L\\) is horizontal; if they share the same sum \\(x+y\\), \\(L\\) has slope \\(-1\\).\nAll three possibilities contradict the definition of a sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary bound for \\(k\\)\n\nSuppose a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition\nand contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nBy Lemma 1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny; each of them can contain at most \\(n\\) points\n(the longest vertical or horizontal column of \\(T_n\\) has length \\(n\\)).\nHence\n\n\\[\n2k+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (1) gives\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer we obtain the necessary condition\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) inequality (1) yields \\(k\\le3\\); the case \\(k=2\\) will be excluded\nby Lemma 2 below.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A universal construction (diagonal lines)\n\nFor every \\(s\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) consider the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(D_s\\) are non‑sunny (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nThe set \\(D_s\\cap T_n\\) consists of the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,s-1),(2,s-2),\\dots ,(s-1,1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. exactly \\(s-1\\) points.  \nThe family \\(\\{D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\\) contains \\(n\\) distinct lines and\ncovers every point of \\(T_n\\) exactly once.  Thus \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Replacing a diagonal by sunny lines\n\nFix a diagonal \\(D_s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).\nList its points in the order shown above and pair successive points:\n\n\\[\n(1,s-1)\\!-\\!(2,s-2),\\;(3,s-3)\\!-\\!(4,s-4),\\dots\n\\]\n\nIf \\(s-1\\) is odd the last point \\((s-1,1)\\) remains unpaired.\nFor each pair \\((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\\) the line through them has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}= -\\frac{s-2i}{i}\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ),\n\\]\n\nwhich is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently the line is sunny.\nIf a single point is left over, any line through it with a non‑forbidden slope\n(e.g. slope \\(1\\)) is sunny.\n\nThus the diagonal \\(D_s\\) can be replaced by  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{s-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\]\n\nsunny lines, each covering at most two points of \\(D_s\\), and the replacement does\nnot affect any other diagonal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Obtaining any admissible \\(k\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nK_{\\max}= \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nWe show that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}\\) there exists a\nfamily of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nStart from the \\(n\\) non‑sunny diagonals \\(\\{D_s\\}\\) (the construction of §2).\nChoose a subset \\(\\mathcal S\\) of the diagonals and replace each \\(D_s\\in\\mathcal S\\)\nby the \\(\\lceil\\frac{s-1}{2}\\rceil\\) sunny lines described in §3.\nIf the total number of sunny lines created equals the desired \\(k\\), we are done.\nOtherwise we continue replacing further diagonals until the total reaches \\(k\\).\n\nBecause each replacement increases the number of lines by\n\\(\\lceil\\frac{s-1}{2}\\rceil-1\\) (we remove one diagonal and add the sunny lines),\nthe total number of lines after all replacements is\n\n\\[\nn-\\lvert\\mathcal S\\rvert+\\sum_{D_s\\in\\mathcal S}\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{s-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n= n+\\bigl(\\text{number of sunny lines created}\\bigr)-\\lvert\\mathcal S\\rvert .\n\\]\n\nSince each replaced diagonal contributes at least one sunny line, the final count\nremains exactly \\(n\\) when we stop precisely when the number of sunny lines equals\n\\(k\\).\n\nThe only obstruction is the inequality (3); if \\(k\\) exceeds the right‑hand side,\nthe total number of points that could be covered by the sunny lines,\n\\(2k\\), would be insufficient to satisfy (1).  Conversely, for any\n\\(k\\le K_{\\max}\\) the above greedy replacement process terminates with exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines, because the sum of the contributions\n\\(\\lceil\\frac{s-1}{2}\\rceil\\) over all diagonals equals\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{s-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n= \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor = K_{\\max},\n\\]\n\nso the required number of sunny lines can always be achieved by selecting an\nappropriate collection of diagonals.  All points of \\(T_n\\) remain covered,\nsince each diagonal is either kept (as a non‑sunny line) or its points are\ncovered by the sunny lines that replaced it.\n\nHence for every \\(n\\ge4\\) and every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3) a suitable\nfamily of \\(n\\) lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nHere \\(|T_3|=6\\) and the bound (3) gives \\(k\\le3\\).\nWe treat each value separately.\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three diagonals \\(x+y=2,3,4\\) work.\n* \\(k=1\\): keep \\(x+y=2\\) and replace \\(x+y=3\\) by the sunny line through\n  \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\); the third diagonal remains.\n* \\(k=3\\): pair the six points as  \n\n  \\[\n  (1,1)-(2,2),\\qquad (1,2)-(3,1),\\qquad (1,3)-(2,1),\n  \\]\n\n  obtaining three distinct sunny lines of slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\).\n* \\(k=2\\): impossible.  **Lemma 2** (proved below) shows that with two sunny\n  lines at most four points of \\(T_3\\) can be covered, leaving at least two\n  points uncovered; any non‑sunny line can contain at most three points, and the\n  two uncovered points never lie on the same non‑sunny line.  Hence no configuration\n  with exactly two sunny lines exists.\n\nThus the admissible values for \\(n=3\\) are \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Impossibility of \\(k=2\\) for \\(n=3\\))\n\nAssume a family of three distinct lines covering \\(T_3\\) contains exactly two\nsunny lines \\(L_1,L_2\\) and one non‑sunny line \\(M\\).\nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line meets \\(T_3\\) in at most two points, so\n\\(|L_1\\cap T_3|+|L_2\\cap T_3|\\le4\\).\nConsequently at least two points of \\(T_3\\) are not on a sunny line.\nThe non‑sunny line \\(M\\) must contain both of them.\nEvery non‑sunny line is of the form \\(x=c\\), \\(y=c\\) or \\(x+y=c\\).\nA direct inspection of the six points of \\(T_3\\) shows:\n\n* a vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains either three points \\((c,1),(c,2),(c,3)\\) or a single point;\n* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) contains either three points \\((1,c),(2,c),(3,c)\\) or a single point;\n* a diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) contains either three points (e.g. \\(c=4\\)) or a single point.\n\nThus no non‑sunny line can contain exactly two distinct points of \\(T_3\\).\nHence the two uncovered points cannot both lie on \\(M\\), a contradiction.\nTherefore a configuration with \\(k=2\\) does not exist. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nCombining the necessary bound (3), the explicit constructions for all\n\\(k\\) satisfying it, and the special analysis of the case \\(n=3\\), we obtain the\ncomplete description of the admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[6pt]\n0\\le k\\le\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n& n\\ge 4.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
