            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[4pt]\n0\\le k\\le n-1,   & n\\ge 4\\text{ even},\\\\[4pt]\n0\\le k\\le n,     & n\\ge 5\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **Upper bounds.**  \n   *Lemma 1.* A sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   *Proof.*  If a sunny line has slope \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and contains the points\n   \\((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\\in T_n\\) with \\(x_1<x_2\\), then\n   \\[\n   y_2-y_1=m(x_2-x_1),\\qquad x_2-x_1\\ge1 .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(|m|\\neq0,1\\), the difference \\(|y_2-y_1|\\) is at least \\(2\\).  \n   Hence between two consecutive points of the line the sum \\(x+y\\) grows by at least\n   \\(3\\).  Starting from the smallest possible sum \\(2\\), after \\(t\\) points the sum is at\n   least \\(2+3(t-1)\\).  The condition \\(x+y\\le n+1\\) forces\n   \\[\n   2+3(t-1)\\le n+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;t\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   ∎  \n\n   *Lemma 2.* A non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   This is immediate because the longest column or row of the triangle has length \\(n\\).\n\n   From Lemma 1 and Lemma 2 we obtain the necessary inequality\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Solving (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2pt]\n   n,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   For even \\(n\\) the bound \\(k=n\\) is impossible because the left‑hand side of (1) would be\n   \\(n\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=n\\cdot\\frac n2<\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\n3. **Construction for odd \\(n\\) (all \\(k\\le n\\)).**  \n   Write \\(n=2m+1\\).  \n   For each integer \\(d\\) with \\(-m\\le d\\le m\\) consider the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_d:\\;y=x+d .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_d\\) is \\(1\\), therefore every \\(L_d\\) is sunny.  \n   Inside the triangle \\(T_n\\) the line \\(L_d\\) contains exactly \\(m+1-|d|\\) points\n   (the points \\((i,i+d)\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le m+1-|d|\\)).  \n   The family \\(\\{L_{-m},L_{-m+1},\\dots ,L_{m}\\}\\) consists of \\(2m+1=n\\) distinct sunny\n   lines and together they cover every point of \\(T_n\\) exactly once; hence \\(k=n\\) is\n   attainable.\n\n   To obtain a configuration with fewer sunny lines we simply replace any\n   \\(t\\;(0\\le t\\le n)\\) of the lines \\(L_d\\) by the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (which is\n   non‑sunny).  The vertical line contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\);\n   the remaining points are still covered by the untouched sunny lines because those\n   points lie on distinct lines \\(L_d\\).  After the replacement we have\n   \\(k=n-t\\) sunny lines and \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines, still exactly \\(n\\) lines in total.\n   Varying \\(t\\) yields every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n4. **Construction for even \\(n\\) (all \\(k\\le n-1\\)).**  \n   Write \\(n=2m\\).  \n   First take the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V:\\;x=1 ,\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   which is non‑sunny and contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\).\n\n   Delete the first column from the triangle; the remaining points form the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T'_m=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le 2m+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Subtract \\(1\\) from the first coordinate; the image is the triangle\n   \\(T_{2m-1}\\) of size \\(2m-1\\).  Inside \\(T_{2m-1}\\) consider the family of\n   sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\n   \\[\n   S_j:\\;y=x+j ,\\qquad j=-(m-1),\\dots ,m-1 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   As in the odd case, each \\(S_j\\) contains exactly \\(m-|j|\\) points of\n   \\(T_{2m-1}\\); the collection \\(\\{S_j\\}_{j=-(m-1)}^{m-1}\\) consists of\n   \\(2m-1=n-1\\) distinct sunny lines and together they cover every point of\n   \\(T_{2m-1}\\) exactly once.  Translating back, the lines \\(S_j\\) cover all points\n   of \\(T'_m\\).  Consequently the set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{V\\}\\cup\\{S_j\\}_{j=-(m-1)}^{m-1}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (\\(V\\)) is non‑sunny;\n   hence \\(k=n-1\\) is attainable.\n\n   To obtain fewer sunny lines we replace any number \\(t\\;(0\\le t\\le n-1)\\) of the\n   sunny lines \\(S_j\\) by the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (which is non‑sunny).  The\n   horizontal line contains the points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\); all points already\n   covered by the replaced sunny lines are still covered because they lie on the\n   horizontal line as well.  After the replacement we have \\(k=n-1-t\\) sunny lines\n   and \\(t+1\\) non‑sunny lines, still exactly \\(n\\) lines in total.  Varying \\(t\\)\n   yields every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n5. **The exceptional case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The set \\(T_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n   \\]\n\n   *Existence.*  \n   – \\(k=0\\): the three diagonals \\(x+y=2,3,4\\) work.  \n   – \\(k=1\\): keep the diagonal \\(x+y=2\\) and replace the diagonal \\(x+y=3\\) by the\n     sunny line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,3)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  Together with the\n     diagonal \\(x+y=4\\) all six points are covered.  \n   – \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines of slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) through the pairs\n     \\((1,1)-(2,2)\\), \\((1,2)-(3,1)\\) and \\((1,3)-(2,1)\\) cover the whole set.\n\n   *Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).*  \n   Suppose a configuration with exactly two sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2\\) and one\n   non‑sunny line \\(M\\) existed.  By Lemma 1 (valid for \\(n=3\\)) each sunny line\n   contains at most two points of \\(T_3\\); therefore \\(L_1\\cup L_2\\) covers at most\n   four points, leaving at least two points uncovered.  Any non‑sunny line is\n   horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\); a direct inspection of the six points\n   shows that **no** such line contains exactly the two uncovered points (every\n   horizontal or vertical line contains either one or three points, and every line\n   of slope \\(-1\\) contains either three points or a single point).  Hence the\n   uncovered points cannot both lie on \\(M\\), a contradiction.  Therefore \\(k=2\\) is\n   impossible.\n\n   Consequently for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the necessary bound (2) with the explicit constructions of Sections 3\n   and 4, and the special analysis of \\(n=3\\), we have completely determined the\n   possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[4pt]\n0\\le k\\le n-1,   & n\\ge4\\text{ even},\\\\[4pt]\n0\\le k\\le n,     & n\\ge5\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verifi