strict to that case.  \n- The example \u201cvertical line \\(x=1\\) contains the whole first column\u201d only works for the specific set of points in the first column; it does **not** contain the points of an arbitrary sunny line. Hence the operation does **not** guarantee preservation of the covering property.  \n\n*Issue:* The reduction step is invalid; it assumes a property that does not hold for most sunny lines.  \n*Classification:* **Critical Error** \u2013 the argument that all intermediate values of \\(k\\) are attainable collapses.\n\n---\n\n### 6. A partition of \\(T_n\\) into \\(n\\) sunny lines (odd \\(n\\))  \n\n> \u201cLet \\(n=2m+1\\;(m\\ge1)\\).  \n> Define the following \\(n\\) lines:  \n> \\[\n> \\begin{aligned}\n> L_{2i}   &:\\; y = -2x + (2m+3+i), && i=0,1,\\dots ,m,\\\\\n> L_{2i+1} &:\\; y =  \\;2x - (i+1),      && i=0,1,\\dots ,m-1 .\n> \\end{aligned}\n> \\tag{3}\n> \\]  \n> *All slopes are \\(\\pm2\\); therefore every line is sunny.*  \n> **Verification that each line meets exactly \\(m+1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).** \u2013 \u2026  \n> \u2013 For an even\u2011indexed line \\(L_{2i}\\) we have the integer points  \n> \\[\n> (x,y)=(1+i+j,\\;2m+2-i-2j),\\qquad j=0,1,\\dots ,m .\n> \\]  \n> \u2026  \n> \u2013 For an odd\u2011indexed line \\(L_{2i+1}\\) the integer points are  \n> \\[\n> (x,y)=(1+i+2j,\\;2j+1),\\qquad j=0,1,\\dots ,m .\n> \\]  \n> **Disjointness.** The two families (3) have distinct constant terms; consequently the sets of points listed above are pairwise disjoint. \u2026  \n> Since we have \\(m+1\\) even\u2011indexed lines and \\(m\\) odd\u2011indexed lines, the total number of covered points is  \n> \\[\n> (m+1)(m+1)+m(m+1)= (2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|T_n|,\n> \\]  \n> i.e. every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one of the lines (3).\u201d\n\n*Verification of slopes:* Both families indeed have slopes \\(-2\\) and \\(+2\\), which are not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus the lines are sunny.\n\n*Verification of the point formulas:*  \n- For an even\u2011indexed line \\(L_{2i}\\) the correct integer points satisfy \\(y=-2x+(2m+3+i)\\). Solving for integer \\(x\\) gives points \\((x, -2x+2m+3+i)\\) with \\(x\\) ranging from \\(1+i\\) up to \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{2m+2+i}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\). The solution\u2019s formula \\((x,y)=(1+i+j,\\,2m+2-i-2j)\\) is **incorrect**: for \\(i=0,\\,j=0\\) it yields \\((1,2m+2)\\), whose sum is \\(2m+3>2m+2\\) and therefore lies outside \\(T_n\\). The correct upper bound on \\(j\\) is not \\(m\\) but \\(\\lfloor m-i/2\\rfloor\\). Consequently the claim that each even\u2011indexed line contains exactly \\(m+1\\) points is false.  \n\n- For an odd\u2011indexed line \\(L_{2i+1}\\) the correct points satisfy \\(y=2x-(i+1)\\). The solution\u2019s formula \\((x,y)=(1+i+2j,\\,2j+1)\\) again fails: for \\(i=0,\\,j=0\\) it gives \\((1,1)\\) (correct), but for \\(j=m\\) it yields \\((1+2m,\\,2m+1)\\) whose sum is \\(2m+2>2m+2\\) when \\(m\\ge1\\). Moreover, the number of admissible \\(j\\) depends on \\(i\\); it is not uniformly \\(m+1\\).  \n\n*Counterexample (n=5, m=2):*  \n- The lines given by (3) are:  \n  \\(L_0: y=-2x+7\\) \u2192 points \\((1,5),(2,3),(3,1)\\) (3 points).  \n  \\(L_1: y=2x-1\\) \u2192 points \\((1,1),(2,3)\\) (2 points, note overlap with \\(L_0\\)).  \n  \\(L_2: y=-2x+8\\) \u2192 points \\((2,4),(3,2)\\) (2 points).  \n  \\(L_3: y=2x-2\\) \u2192 point \\((2,2)\\) (1 point).  \n  \\(L_4: y=-2x+9\\) \u2192 points \\((3,3),(4,1)\\) (2 points).  \n  The claimed disjointness fails (e.g. \\((2,3)\\) lies on both \\(L_0\\) and \\(L_1\\)), and many points of \\(T_5\\) such as \\((1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(4,2),(5,1)\\) are not covered at all.  \n\n*Issue:* The construction does **not** partition \\(T_n\\) into \\(n\\) sunny lines; the point enumeration is wrong, the sets are not disjoint, and the total count is incorrect.  \n*Classification:* **Critical Error** \u2013 the entire odd\u2011\\(n\\) construction collapses.\n\n---\n\n### 7. The case \\(n=3\\)  \n\n> \u20