eduction step is unsound).\n\n---\n\n### 9. Conclusion  \n\n> \u201cThe necessary condition (2) is also sufficient, with the only exceptional behaviour occurring at \\(n=3\\).  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the boxed answer.\u201d\n\n*Verification:* Since the sufficiency part (the constructions) is invalid, the conclusion does not follow. The answer claimed in the boxed statement is not established.\n\n*Issue:* The final claim is unsupported.  \n*Classification:* **Critical Error** \u2013 the proof of sufficiency fails.\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment:**  \nThe solution\u2019s core bound on the number of points per sunny line is not proved (and the provided justification is false). The reduction argument that would allow any intermediate number of sunny lines is mathematically invalid. Most seriously, the explicit construction claimed for odd \\(n\\) (which would yield \\(k=n\\)) is incorrect; it fails already for the smallest odd case \\(n=5\\). Consequently the answer \u201c\\(k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\) for \\(n=3\\); \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) for even \\(n\\ge4\\); \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) for odd \\(n\\ge5\\)\u201d is not proven and, in fact, is false for at least \\(n=5\\). The solution therefore does **not** meet the standards of a correct IMO\u2011level proof."

