            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[4pt]\n0\\le k\\le n-1,   & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Maximum points on a sunny line.**  \n   Let a sunny line have reduced direction vector \\((q,p)\\) with\n   \\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\) (otherwise the line would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   The step from one lattice point of the line to the next is \\((q,p)\\); the sum \\(a+b\\) therefore changes by \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\) (the case \\(|p+q|=1\\) would force \\(|p|=|q|=1\\) and then \\(p=-q\\), which is forbidden).  \n   Consequently a sunny line can meet at most  \n\n   \\[\n   s_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n3. **A necessary inequality.**  \n   A non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   If a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, then\n\n   \\[\n   k\\,s_n+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2pt]\n   n-1, & n\\text{ odd and }n\\ge5,\\\\[2pt]\n   3,   & n=3 .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   (For \\(n=3\\) we have \\(s_3=2\\) and (2) yields \\(k\\le3\\).)\n\n4. **The easy construction \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   D_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n   \\]\n\n   are non‑sunny, are distinct, and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Hence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n5. **A universal construction with exactly one non‑sunny line.**  \n   *Vertical line* \\(V:\\;x=1\\) is non‑sunny and contains the whole first column of \\(T_n\\), i.e. the \\(n\\) points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  \n   Removing this column leaves the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T'_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which is precisely the translate \\(\\{(a-1,b)\\mid (a,b)\\in T_{\\,n-1}\\}\\).  \n   Thus covering \\(T_n\\) is equivalent to covering \\(T_{\\,n-1}\\) together with the vertical line \\(V\\).\n\n6. **Inductive partition of \\(T_m\\) into \\(m\\) lines, exactly \\(m-1\\) of them sunny.**  \n   We prove by induction on \\(m\\ge3\\) that for every \\(m\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) of \\(m\\) distinct lines satisfying  \n\n   * (i) the union of the lines is \\(T_m\\);  \n   * (ii) exactly one line of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) is non‑sunny (it will be the vertical line \\(x=1\\));  \n   * (iii) the remaining \\(m-1\\) lines are sunny.\n\n   *Base \\(m=3\\).*  \n   Take  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &V:\\;x=1,\\\\\n   &L_1:\\;y=2x-3,\\\\\n   &L_2:\\;y=2x-1 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   \\(V\\) covers \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\); \\(L_1\\) covers \\((2,1)\\); \\(L_2\\) covers \\((2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n   All six points of \\(T_3\\) are covered and the two slant lines have slope \\(2\\) (sunny).\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume \\(\\mathcal L_{m}\\) exists for some \\(m\\ge3\\).  \n   To obtain \\(\\mathcal L_{m+1}\\) we start from \\(\\mathcal L_{m}\\) (which covers \\(T_m\\)) and add the new points that appear in \\(T_{m+1}\\) but not in \\(T_m\\).  \n   Those new points are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   U_{m+1}=\\{(1,m+1)\\}\\cup\\{(a,\\,m+2-a)\\mid a=2,3,\\dots ,m+1\\}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The first point \\((1,m+1)\\) already lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\).  \n   The remaining \\(m\\) points of \\(U_{m+1}\\) lie on the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{m+1}:\\;y=-\\,x+(m+2),\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   which has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore **non‑sunny**.  \n   Replace the unique non‑sunny line of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) (the vertical line) by the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V:\\;x=1\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad S_{m+1} .\n   \\]\n\n   The family now contains \\(m+2\\) lines, of which exactly two are non‑sunny.  \n   To restore the required number \\(m+1\\) of lines we merge one of the sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) with the vertical line \\(V\\):  \n   the vertical line already covers all points of \\(V\\); we may simply discard any sunny line of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) (its points are already covered by the other lines because the set \\(U_{m+1}\\) is disjoint from the points of that sunny line).  \n   Consequently we obtain a family of \\(m+1\\) lines that covers \\(T_{m+1}\\) and has exactly one non‑sunny line (the vertical line \\(x=1\\)).  \n   The other \\(m\\) lines are sunny, completing the induction.\n\n   By induction, for every \\(m\\ge3\\) there exists a covering of \\(T_m\\) by \\(m\\) lines with **exactly one non‑sunny line**.  \n\n7. **Realising every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(n\\ge4\\).  \n   From step 6 we have a covering \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) of \\(T_n\\) consisting of one non‑sunny line and \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \n   To obtain a covering with fewer sunny lines we simply replace any sunny line of \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) by a non‑sunny line that already contains al