(horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   If a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, then  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\,s_n+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (3) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1, & n\\ge 4,\\\\[2pt]\n   3,   & n=3 .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   (For \\(n=3\\) we have \\(s_3=2\\) and (3) yields \\(k\\le3\\).)\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n   Suppose \\(k=n\\). Then (3) must be an equality, so every sunny line must contain exactly \\(s_n\\) points and the families of points must be pairwise disjoint.  \n   Equality in (1) forces \\(|p+q|=2\\) and both \\(p,q\\) positive; the only primitive vector with these properties is \\((1,1)\\), i.e. the line has slope \\(1\\).  \n   A line of slope \\(1\\) meets at most \\(s_n\\) points, and it meets exactly \\(s_n\\) points only for the line \\(y=x\\). Consequently at most **one** of the \\(n\\) sunny lines can contain \\(s_n\\) points; the remaining sunny lines contain at most \\(s_n-1\\) points, making the left‑hand side of (3) strictly smaller than \\(|T_n|\\).  \n   Hence \\(k=n\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n5. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   D_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n   \\]\n\n   are non‑sunny, distinct, and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Thus \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n6. **Construction for \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) (any \\(n\\ge3\\)).**  \n   We first build a configuration with **exactly one** non‑sunny line and \\(n-1\\) sunny lines; afterwards we replace sunny lines by additional non‑sunny lines to obtain any smaller value of \\(k\\).\n\n   *Step 6.1 – a base configuration.*  \n   For a given \\(n\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &V:\\;x=1 \\qquad\\text{(vertical, non‑sunny)},\\\\[2pt]\n   &L_i:\\;y=i\\,x-(i-1