            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[4pt]\n0\\le k\\le n-1,      & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Maximum points on a sunny line.**  \n   A sunny line has equation \\(p\\,x-q\\,y=c\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\neq0\\) and\n   \\(p\\neq -q\\).  \n   Between two consecutive lattice points on the line the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by\n   \\(|p+q|\\ge 1\\).  \n   Starting from the smallest possible sum \\(2\\) and never exceeding \\(n+1\\),\n   the number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can lie on a sunny line is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   s_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Necessary inequality for the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.**  \n   A non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) contains at most\n   \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   With \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\,s_n+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1, & n\\ge 4,\\\\[2pt]\n   3,   & n=3 .\n   \\end{cases}\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n\n   *For \\(n=4\\).*  Here \\(s_4=\\lfloor5/2\\rfloor=2\\); the left‑hand side of (2) with\n   \\(k=4\\) equals \\(4\\cdot2=8<|T_4|=10\\).  Hence \\(k=4\\) is impossible.\n\n   *For odd \\(n\\ge5\\).*  Equality in (2) would be required, so every sunny line\n   must contain exactly \\(s_n=(n+1)/2\\) points and the \\(n\\) families of points\n   would be pairwise disjoint.  Because a sunny line is not vertical, its points\n   have distinct \\(x\\)‑coordinates; consequently each sunny line uses exactly\n   \\(s_n\\) different \\(x\\)-values.  The total number of distinct \\(x\\)-values\n   used would be \\(n\\,s_n>n\\), contradicting the fact that the only possible\n   \\(x\\)-coordinates are \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\).  Hence \\(k=n\\) cannot occur for any\n   \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n   Therefore the maximal possible number of sunny lines is \\(n-1\\) for every\n   \\(n\\ge3\\); the case \\(n=3\\) is exceptional because \\(s_3=2\\) and\n   \\(k=3\\) satisfies (2) with equality.\n\n5. **Construction with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines (all \\(n\\ge3\\)).**  \n\n   *Non‑sunny line.*  Take the diagonal  \n\n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  Choose a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of the set\n   \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with no fixed point (a derangement; such a permutation\n   exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\)).  For each \\(i\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\text{ the line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(1,\\sigma(i)).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-\\sigma(i)}{i-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\);\n   hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  The lines \\(L_i\\) are distinct because two\n   different indices give different unordered pairs of points.\n\n   **Points on \\(L_i\\).**  \n   Any integer point on \\(L_i\\) has the form  \n\n   \\[\n   (i+(i-1)t,\\;1+(1-\\sigma(i))t),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(t=0\\) we obtain \\((i,1)\\); for \\(t=1\\) the second coordinate equals\n   \\(2-\\sigma(i)\\le0\\) (since \\(\\sigma(i)\\ge2\\)), which is not a positive\n   integer.  For \\(t\\le-1\\) the first coordinate becomes \\(\\le0\\).  Hence the\n   only points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(L_i\\) are the two endpoints \\((i,1)\\) and\n   \\((1,\\sigma(i))\\).\n\n   Consequently the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together cover exactly the\n   \\(2(n-1)\\) points of the first row and the first column (except the corner\n   \\((1,1)\\)).  The diagonal \\(D\\) contains **all** points of \\(T_n\\); in\n   particular it also contains the two points of each \\(L_i\\).  Thus the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_n=\\{D\\}\\cup\\{L_i\\mid i=2,\\dots ,n\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of which are sunny,\n   and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n6. **Obtaining any smaller number of sunny lines.**  \n   Starting from \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) we may replace a sunny line \\(L_i\\) by any\n   other diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\), \\(c\\neq n+1\\)).  Such a diagonal\n   is non‑sunny and still contains the two points \\((i,1)\\) and\n   \\((1,\\sigma(i))\\) that were the only points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(L_i\\); therefore\n   the covering property is preserved while the number of sunny lines is reduced\n   by one.  Repeating this operation we obtain configurations with any prescribed\n   number  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n7. **The case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The general construction above works for \\(n=3\\) and yields \\(k=2\\).  \n   To exhibit all admissible values we give explicit families:\n\n   *\\(k=0\\):* \\(\\{x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4\\}\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\):* \\(\\{x+y=4,\\;x=1,\\;y=1\\}\\) (one non‑sunny diagonal and two\n   non‑sunny axes).\n\n   *\\(k=2\\):* \\(\\{x+y=4,\\;x=1,\\;y=2x-1\\}\\) (two sunny lines \\(y=2x-1\\) and\n   \\(y=x\\) together with the diagonal).\n\n   *\\(k=3\\):* \\(\\{y=x,\\;y=2x-1,\\;y=2x-3\\}\\); each of the three lines is sunny\n   and a direct substitution shows that the six points of \\(T_3\\) are covered.\n\n   All four families consist of three distinct lines and satisfy the required\n   covering condition, proving that every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) is attainable for\n   \\(n=3\\).\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (2) together with the impossibility proof for \\(k=n\\) shows that\n   no configuration can have more than the numbers of sunny lines listed in\n   (3).  The explicit constructions of Sections 5–7 realise every admissible\n   value.  Hence the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly the\n   one stated in the boxed answer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[4pt]\n0\\le k\\le n-1,      & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
