n   For \\(n\\ge4\\) the right\u2013hand side of (2) is strictly smaller than \\(n-1\\);\n   a short computation shows that it equals \\(n-2\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2\\qquad (n\\ge4).                                   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n=3\\) inequality (1) yields \\(k\\le3\\).\n\n4. **Construction for the extremal value \\(k=n-2\\) ( \\(n\\ge4\\) ).**  \n   *Diagonal line*  \n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=n+1\n   \\]\n   is non\u2011sunny and contains exactly the \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\) whose\n   coordinates sum to \\(n+1\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines*  \n   For each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{i}:\\text{ the line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(i+1,n-i).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The second point satisfies \\((i+1)+(n-i)=n+1\\), so it lies on \\(D\\).\n   The slope of \\(S_{i}\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n-i-1}{\\,i+1-i\\,}=n-i-1\\neq0,\\infty,-1,\n   \\]\n\n   hence every \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny.  The lines \\(S_{i}\\) are distinct because\n   they have different slopes.\n\n   *Coverage.*  \n\n   * First row.  For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) the point \\((i,1)\\) lies on\n     \\(S_{i}\\); the points \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) lie on the horizontal\n     line  \n\n     \\[\n     H:\\;y=1,\n     \\]\n\n     which is non\u2011sunny.\n\n   * Interior points.  Let \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) with \\(b\\ge2\\).  \n     If \\(a\\ge n-1\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b\\le2\\); such a point is\n     \\((n-1,2)\\) or \\((n,2)\\) and both belong to the vertical line  \n\n     \\[\n     V:\\;x=n .\n     \\]\n\n     If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n-1\\).  The point \\((a,b)\\) lies on\n     the vertical line \\(x=a\\); this line is non\u2011sunny and contains at most\n     \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).  All these vertical lines together with\n     \\(H\\) and \\(V\\) cover every interior point.\n\n   Consequently the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{n}= \\{D\\}\\cup\\{H\\}\\cup\\{V\\}\\cup\\{S_{i}\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-2\\